<script>
	import Callout from '$lib/components/Callout.svelte';
</script>

When requests are independent, running them one after another just wastes wall-clock.
You don't hand-write a thread pool, though — you declare dependencies with
`depends_on`, and ApiTool computes the execution **waves** with a topological sort.
Independent requests in the same wave run concurrently; extracted variables flow
forward to the next wave.

## The collection

`Create user` and `Create product` don't depend on each other, so they belong in the
same wave. `Place order` needs both, so it waits.

```yaml
# collections/order-graph.yaml
name: Order graph
requests:
  - name: Create user
    request:
      method: POST
      url: "{{base_url}}/users"
      body: { name: "{{$faker.fullName}}" }
    extract:
      user_id: "$.id"

  - name: Create product
    request:
      method: POST
      url: "{{base_url}}/products"
      body: { name: "Widget", price: 19.99 }
    extract:
      product_id: "$.id"

  - name: Place order
    depends_on: [Create user, Create product]
    request:
      method: POST
      url: "{{base_url}}/orders"
      body:
        user_id: "{{user_id}}"
        product_id: "{{product_id}}"
    assertions:
      status: 201
```

## Preview the plan — without sending a single request

`--show-dependencies` prints the computed waves; combine it with `--dry-run` to
resolve every variable and preview URLs and bodies with zero network traffic. A
cycle (A→B→A) is rejected at parse time with a clear error.

```bash
apitest run collections/order-graph.yaml --show-dependencies --dry-run
```

```
  Wave 1: [Create user, Create product] → 2 concurrent
  Wave 2: [Place order] → 1 sequential

Total waves: 2
Maximum parallelism: 2 requests per wave
Expected speedup: 1.5x
```

## Run it for real

```bash
apitest run collections/order-graph.yaml --parallel
```

```
Collection: Order graph

Wave 1 (2 concurrent):
  ✓ Create user  201  88ms
  ✓ Create product  201  91ms

Wave 2 (1 concurrent):
  ✓ Place order  201  102ms

────────────────────────────────
  3 request(s): 3 passed, 0 failed (193ms)
  Waves: 2, Max parallelism: 2
  Speedup: 1.7x
```

<Callout type="tip">
	This is ApiTool's real parallel output, captured from a run — the
	<code>Speedup</code> line corresponds to
	<code>.parallel_execution.speedup_factor</code> in <code>--format json</code>.
	Waves are computed from data flow: <code>Place order</code> uses values extracted by
	the first two requests, so it lands in wave 2.
</Callout>

<Callout type="note">
	Parallelism is opt-in: without <code>--parallel</code>, the collection runs
	sequentially regardless of <code>depends_on</code>. The dependency graph is always
	respected — the flag only decides whether independent requests overlap.
</Callout>
